State the physical assumptions behind a lumped circuit (@physics-circuit-meaning: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../electromagnetism/currents/Circuit-approximations-sources-resistance-and-power.lingua#r_0Y05GYZKN18BT1FY2GGQ6476W2") { r_001A7088BC509FE999669C908D
A circuit description replaces extended electromagnetic behavior by a network of elements and ports. To use it, identify the conductors, reference nodes, directions, element relations and initial stored quantities. Assume that propagation, neglected field coupling and unmodeled inductive or radiative effects are small enough over the times and geometry under study. A drawn wire is not proof of an exactly equal potential in a real fast process; a component label is not a constitutive law without a domain.

For an ideal resistor take v=Ri, R>0 constant within the model. Then absorbed power is vi=Ri²=v²/R≥0. The electrical input becomes other forms of energy; it is not charge being consumed. If temperature changes appreciably or the relation is nonlinear, use the changed material response rather than assuming the same R. The empirical usefulness of Ohmic behavior does not make it a universal relation for all matter or all operating conditions.

For node potentials V_j defined consistently in a quasistatic network, every branch voltage is a difference. Summing such differences around a closed sequence telescopes to zero. This algebra does not establish that a single scalar node potential accounts for every induced field in a time-dependent magnetic environment; induction must be included in an extended element or field account. Current balance at a junction separately requires negligible changing charge there or an explicit storage term. Voltage compatibility, charge balance and element physics are different premises.

As an elementary source model, let an ideal source of voltage E drive a series resistance r and load R, both positive. The steady current is E/(r+R); the terminal/load voltage is IR. Ideal-source delivered power EI equals I²r+I²R. Opening the load gives zero current and terminal voltage E in the limiting model; it does not imply that a source stores no available energy. The ideal source constrains a port relation and supplies work; its internal chemical or other conversion mechanism is not explained by that symbol.

Now define the source-free system used next. Capacitor C₁ has its lower plate joined to the lower plate of C₂ at reference node 0. Their upper nodes 1 and 2 are connected by a resistor R. Ignore leakage, stray coupling and wire storage, and hold C₁,C₂,R positive and constant. Define v₁=V₁−V₀, v₂=V₂−V₀ and I=(v₁−v₂)/R from node 1 to node 2. The charge equations are C₁v₁′=−I and C₂v₂′=I. The upper-node charge sum C₁v₁+C₂v₂ is conserved, while the whole two-capacitor arrangement remains net neutral in the ideal separated-charge description. Stored energy changes at rate v₁(−I)+v₂I=−I²R.

**Work and reading:** derive those equations from separate node boundaries and the port sign convention, then reverse every chosen current direction and recover unchanged physical predictions. Reconstruct the existing source/load example in [Circuit approximations, sources and power](../electromagnetism/currents/Circuit-approximations-sources-resistance-and-power.lingua#r_0Y05GYZKN18BT1FY2GGQ6476W2) without copying a remembered answer. Read [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q) 9.4, 9.5 and 10.1–10.3 progressively. General field-to-circuit justification, magnetic induction and practical source/device realization remain later work; the current model supplies a bounded question about interaction, conservation and evidence.

**Continue:** [Circuit approximations, sources and power](../electromagnetism/currents/Circuit-approximations-sources-resistance-and-power.lingua#r_0Y05GYZKN18BT1FY2GGQ6476W2).

